## Know which file goes where

| File | Location | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `campfile.yml` | Application repository root. | Application identity, environments, platform metadata, and integration settings. Use the version supported by its consumer. |
| Enrollment `<app_name>.yaml` | Configuration repository: `config/teams/<team>/bitrise/apps/`. | Create-only request for a new Bitrise app and approved bindings. No secrets or credential references. |
| `bitrise.yml` | Application root where required by the approved template. | Workflow/pipeline configuration; use the governed template and permitted extension points. |
| `dangerfile.ts` | Approved trusted review-tooling checkout. | PR checks. Do not execute unreviewed PR-controlled tooling with the protected bot credential. |
| `saucelabs.request.yml` | Application repository root. | Enterprise device-testing request metadata. |
| `.sauce/*.yml` | Application repository. | Supported native test-runner configurations. The selected framework determines its actual format. |

The application source branch is separate from configuration `develop` and `main`. Record which repository each commit belongs to.

## Record what will be built

From the application checkout:

```bash
git status --short --branch
git branch --show-current
git rev-parse HEAD
```

Record the source branch and exact commit. Uncommitted files are not part of the remote build. Keep these identities separate:

| Identity | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- |
| Application commit | Source actually built. |
| Enrollment PR head | Configuration revision reviewed and validated. |
| Protected configuration merge | Authorized input that starts live apply. |
| Build/route and destination version | Executed candidate and the version users must receive. |

The enrollment request selects a branch. Verify the commit actually used by each build if that branch moved.

## Use the four governed routes

| Route | Output in the supplied Build & Release Kit | Intended audience |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `qa_android` | Private Android APK. | Approved QA users. |
| `qa_ios` | Private iOS IPA. | Approved QA users. |
| `uat_android` | Private Android APK. | Approved UAT users. |
| `uat_ios` | Private iOS IPA. | Approved UAT users. |

Verify Android and iOS independently. The approved catalog defines native-only applicability and target mappings. A route name is not automatically a Gradle task or Xcode scheme.

Google Play AAB delivery and App Store Connect/TestFlight delivery use their approved destination workflows. QA/UAT success does not authorize public production release. The supplied policy requires TGRC approval before UAT/Production deployment.

## Understand ownership

| Owner | Responsibility |
| :--- | :--- |
| Developer | Source, CAMP configuration, tests, native targets, and request PR. |
| Reviewer | Intended changes, current-revision checks, access assignments, and required approvals. |
| Platform Engineering | Provisioning, templates, runners, protected credentials, integrations, and recovery. |
| QA/release owner | Required device coverage, intended audience, applicable authorization, and acceptance evidence. |

**Enroll through GitOps.** The new handbook contains console onboarding instructions and a GitOps-only mandate. Follow the governed process established by the earlier Build & Release Kit and workshop; underlying console settings remain platform-owned operations.

## Read results correctly

| Result | What it proves |
| :--- | :--- |
| Validation passed | That revision passed the configured validation. No live app is created by local checks or PR preview. |
| App created | The resource exists. It does not prove all bindings, builds, or delivery succeeded. |
| Build passed | The configured work completed. Inspect required test/security evidence separately. |
| Upload confirmed | The destination received an identified artifact. Processing, signing, enablement, and access can remain incomplete. |
| Delivery verified | The exact version is available to the approved audience through the intended route. |

The release evidence milestones are **BuildStarted → BuildSuccessful → ArtifactValidated → Uploaded → Signed → Enabled → Assigned & UserVisible**. They describe required evidence; actual signing order depends on the destination. Installation/launch is verified separately when required.

The workshop's historical canary confirmed QA Android delivery, while QA iOS built successfully but failed downstream signing. UAT publication was unconfirmed. The overall result was **partial failure**, not four successful releases.

