## 1. Write the request

**Configuration repository file:** `config/teams/sample-team/bitrise/apps/sample-mobile.yaml`.

```yaml
app_name: sample-mobile
team: sample-team
repo_url: https://git.example.invalid/mobile/sample-mobile.git
source_branch: REPLACE_WITH_CATALOG_REQUIRED_BRANCH
platform: react-native
template_id: REPLACE_WITH_APPROVED_TEMPLATE_ID
template_version: "REPLACE_WITH_EXACT_APPROVED_SEMVER"
access_groups:
  - REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWED_ACCESS_GROUP
optional_users: []
```

| Rule | Required value |
| :--- | :--- |
| Path | Lowercase path from the Build & Release Kit; team directory matches `team`. |
| Filename | `app_name` plus `.yaml`. |
| Source | Actual private repository and catalog-required branch, without embedded credentials. |
| Platform | `react-native`, `android`, or `ios`, with a compatible approved template. |
| Template | Exact approved immutable version; historical examples are not current pins. |
| Access | Reviewed group membership; optional user emails receive only the contract's viewer-level access. |

No passwords, keys, certificates, profiles, credential URLs, vault references, or lifecycle mutations (`action: update`, `delete: true`, `operation: reconcile`). CAMP and enrollment are different contracts. Record the prepared app commit in evidence; no `source_commit` request field was supplied.

## 2. Branch from configuration develop and validate

Start in a clean configuration checkout. These commands assume `origin` and local `develop` exist:

```bash
git status --short --branch
git fetch origin
git switch develop
git merge --ff-only origin/develop
git switch -c feature/enroll-sample-mobile
npm test
npm run lint
```

Use the repository's approved branch naming and any additional validator. Checks cover names/path, allowed fields, approved catalog compatibility, forbidden content/secrets, and read-only repository/branch accessibility in CI. A local pass does not prove provider access or live app creation.

```bash
MOBILE_REQUEST_FILE='config/teams/sample-team/bitrise/apps/sample-mobile.yaml'
git add -- "$MOBILE_REQUEST_FILE"
git diff --cached --check
git diff --cached -- "$MOBILE_REQUEST_FILE"
git commit -m "chore(bitrise): request sample mobile enrollment"
git push --set-upstream origin HEAD
git rev-parse HEAD
```

Replace sample identities. Open a request PR to **configuration `develop`** with source identity, template/version, access review, and validation results.

## 3. Inspect the exact PR preview

The authorized Vela operator uses the approved VPN/access path. Compare the current PR head with the preview's app/team, source branch, template/version, and groups. Wait for required checks/review; changes to the PR need current-revision validation.

Local validation and PR preview do not provision an app. A bypass does not make a pending/failed check pass.

**Attach screenshot — sanitized Vela preview matched to the request PR head.**

## 4. Promote to protected main

Merge the reviewed request to `develop`, then open a promotion PR from `develop` to `main`. Review the **whole promotion diff**, including other accumulated requests. Platform Engineering completes the protected merge under current branch rules.

Record the request head, promotion head, actual protected merge commit, live-apply reference, and correlation ID. These are separate from the app source commit.

## 5. Inspect live provisioning

| Platform operation | Expected state |
| :--- | :--- |
| App creation | Correct Enterprise workspace, private app, unique intended identity. |
| Source binding | Approved SSH read-only deploy key or authorized service integration; correct repository/branch. |
| Scanner/toolchain | Actual source layout and compatible stack; a scanner recommendation is not an approved template change. |
| Webhooks | Approved authenticated push/PR integration and route mapping, with duplicate-event handling. |
| Template | Requested approved immutable catalog version. |
| Credentials/access | Platform-owned signing bindings and only reviewed groups/users. |
| Initial execution | Actual build commits, artifacts, and independent route outcomes. |

Never treat app creation as successful distribution. The source branch can move; inspect the actual commit each build used.

## 6. Stop on partial or uncertain state

Preserve the app, source access, credentials, artifacts, destination versions, and completed routes. Do not reenroll, replay the merge, delete resources, or repeat an uncertain write. Platform Engineering reconciles supported GET/readbacks and resumes only reviewed remaining work.

