### Verify a single-current-signer APK

**File: `scripts/ci/verify-apk-signer.sh`.** The expected fingerprint comes from the protected route inventory, not from the artifact itself.

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
set +x

: "${MOBILE_APKSIGNER:?Approved apksigner executable path is required}"
: "${MOBILE_SIGNED_APK:?Signed APK path is required}"
: "${MOBILE_EXPECTED_CERT_SHA256:?Approved signer fingerprint is required}"
test -x "$MOBILE_APKSIGNER"
test -s "$MOBILE_SIGNED_APK"

expected="$(printf '%s' "$MOBILE_EXPECTED_CERT_SHA256" | tr -d '[:space:]:' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
[[ "$expected" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{64}$ ]] || exit 1

# verify fails on invalid APK signatures; capture certificate diagnostics locally.
verification="$("$MOBILE_APKSIGNER" verify --verbose --print-certs "$MOBILE_SIGNED_APK")"
count="$(printf '%s\n' "$verification" | awk '/^Signer #[0-9]+ certificate SHA-256 digest:/ { n++ } END { print n+0 }')"
[[ "$count" == 1 ]] || exit 1
actual="$(printf '%s\n' "$verification" | awk -F': ' '/^Signer #1 certificate SHA-256 digest:/ { print tolower($2) }')"
[[ "$actual" == "$expected" ]] || exit 1
printf 'APK signature and configured signer check passed.\n'
shasum -a 256 "$MOBILE_SIGNED_APK"
```

All `MOBILE_...` inputs are example-owned bindings. The parser deliberately fails on missing/unexpected/multiple signer output. Key rotation/lineage needs an approved verification policy. `apksigner` checks APKs, not AABs; AABs require approved bundle/JAR-signature and expected-certificate validation. [Android apksigner](https://developer.android.com/tools/apksigner) defines the tool.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Confirm bundle/extension IDs, team, entitlements, certificate/private key, provisioning profiles, Xcode, archive scheme, and intended distribution method. API access alone does not establish signing readiness.

```yaml
- xcode-archive@5:
    title: Archive approved store configuration
    inputs:
      - project_path: $BITRISE_PROJECT_PATH
      - scheme: $BITRISE_SCHEME
      - distribution_method: app-store
      - automatic_code_signing: api-key
```

This is a **store** archive excerpt using the source major; the approved template supplies the compatible Apple connection and pin. Enterprise QA/UAT can require a different export method. Do not copy `app-store` onto every route.

For an already extracted, trusted device `.app`:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
set +x
: "${MOBILE_SIGNED_APP:?Exact extracted signed device app required}"
test -d "$MOBILE_SIGNED_APP"
codesign --verify --deep --strict --verbose=2 "$MOBILE_SIGNED_APP"
codesign --display --verbose=4 "$MOBILE_SIGNED_APP"
```

Inspect the expected identity/team, nested code, entitlements, and profile compatibility under the approved policy. A valid/ad hoc Simulator signature is not distribution evidence.

When an embedded profile is expected:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
set +x
umask 077
: "${MOBILE_SIGNED_APP:?Extracted signed device app required}"
mkdir -p .tmp/signing
profile_output="$(mktemp .tmp/signing/profile.XXXXXX)"
trap 'rm -f "$profile_output"' EXIT
security cms -D -i "$MOBILE_SIGNED_APP/embedded.mobileprovision" > "$profile_output"
/usr/libexec/PlistBuddy -c 'Print :ExpirationDate' "$profile_output"
```

This displays expiry; it does not automatically compare dates or validate every entitlement. Keep decoded profiles restricted. See [Apple profile structure](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/technotes/tn3125-inside-code-signing-provisioning-profiles).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

React Native uses separate Android and iOS signing. Credentials do not belong in JavaScript bundles, Redux state, Metro configuration, or application logs.

```bash
set -euo pipefail
: "${MOBILE_SIGNED_ARTIFACT:?Exact signed native candidate required}"
test -s "$MOBILE_SIGNED_ARTIFACT"
shasum -a 256 "$MOBILE_SIGNED_ARTIFACT"
```

Run the corresponding native identity verification first. This command checks size/hash only. Keep one source relationship with independent platform artifacts and signatures.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 3. Verify downstream signing separately

App Management can sign after ingestion. Match its input artifact, operation, resulting version, expected signer, and resulting hash/lineage. A passed Bitrise iOS build does not prove downstream signing succeeded.

The workshop reference reported `distribution_signing_failed` after QA iOS built. Preserve the existing destination object and ask the owner to reconcile; the evidence does not establish a certificate root cause or authorize replacement.

## 4. Handle credentials and cleanup through owners

| Event | Required handling |
| :--- | :--- |
| Expiry/renewal | Owner-reviewed rotation with affected route validation and install/update continuity checks. |
| Exposure | Incident containment and authorized revocation/rotation; do not copy the leaked value into reports. |
| Missing/wrong binding | Fail explicitly. No debug fallback, TLS bypass, or guessed key regeneration. |
| Temporary files/keychains | Restricted permissions; cleanup on success/failure; remove only operation-owned resources. |
| Unknown external result | Stop writes, preserve resources, and reconcile read-only. |

Never cache or publish credentials, signing keychains, private URLs, or decoded profiles. Secret masking and file deletion are not universal leak-prevention/erasure guarantees.

